okay let's get into this week's audience  questions now the first question I'll  tell you what the question is and then  I'm gonna sort of give a caveat before I  answer it the question is hey David do  you think it makes sense to raise kids  gender and sex orientation neutral so  that they aren't influenced in any way  as to what they want to be or who they  want to be I'm going to answer this  question as if it's like an in good  faith question but it also sort of feels  like a straw man in the sense that there  are increasingly arguments being made  against positions that nobody really  holds or that significant portions of  the population don't actually hold and  this is sort of one of them that comes  from the anti-lgbt sort of transphobic  wing of the political spectrum so I'm  gonna pretend that this is a real  question which is listen some people are  gay lesbian bisexual some people end up  choosing to live their life in line with  the gender identity that they feel is  actually correct which may not match the  physical sex that they were born into um  and since that applies to some portion  of the population should parents raise  children in a completely gender-neutral  and sexual orientation neutral way the  answer is no okay I mean listen let's  see it looks at sort of just look at it  empirically most people are heterosexual  and most people end up feeling as though  they were born into a body that has the  right genitalia that is the right sex  and gender that it is a match that their  identity is a match with the body into  which they were born so there's no real  empirical basis upon which we should say  the logical path forward is for parents  to sort of shelter their kids from the  fact that they are treating them if they  were born with male genitalia as if  their gender identity for life is going  to be male or the same with  with female genitalia there is no reason  why parents should raise their children  with sort of complete neutrality in  terms of behavior about that because  statistically speaking for the most part  if you treat a child born with male  genitalia as having male gender identity  or a child born with female genitalia as  having female gender identity that's  going to be the way that their life  shakes out so  I don't think there's really any  sensible reason why we should say  parents should raise their children  pretending that it's just as likely that  they would end up if they were born with  male sex organs wanting to live as a  female rather than male because the  statistics simply don't bear it out not  even close  what I think does make sense is to as a  general sort of practice of parenting  not hide from your kids that there are a  variety of sexual orientations that  transgender is a thing you don't have to  use those terms necessarily since the  the terms don't make that much sense the  little kids but you can explain that  people have feel different ways  sometimes or whatever language makes  sense it's not really for me to come up  with exactly what the language would be  but the point is you want to foster an  environment where your kids are going to  number one be respectful and treat other  people who are members of the LGBTQ  community with respect and with decency  and the way everybody sort of deserves  and to be treated and should be treated  but also in the event that your child  ultimately expresses these feelings or  has these feelings internally that they  are going to be comfortable telling you  that that's the case that they are going  to be comfortable living as who they  ultimately turn out to be so for me it's  really a straw man to say if some people  are going to ultimately say that their  gender expression doesn't match their  birth sex or some people are going to  end up saying well actually I'm  I'm physically attracted to other men or  whatever that every parent now needs to  sort of raise their children insulated  from a sort of sort of but interacting  with them as if we are placing on them  what the most likely sexual orientation  or gender is that's a straw man I've not  actually heard anyone seriously make  that argument I'm not saying it doesn't  exist everything exists some people are  making any argument you can imagine  basically but let's just be parents who  are raising children that are going to  number one exists in the world treating  everybody with respect and kindness and  number two not feeling as though they  are unable to or scared of or unwilling  to tell their parents what it is that's  going on with them so if you're asking  the question honestly  then that's my answer if you're asking  the question and bad faith then I  probably shouldn't have taken five five  minutes to answer it but I'm going to  assume that the question was being asked  in good faith
 

